"""Owner-facing prognosis copy: useful clinical orientation without mini literature reviews."""

DOG = {
'lymphoma': 'Lymphoma is usually treatable but not usually cured. For dogs with common high-grade nodal B-cell lymphoma treated with CHOP chemotherapy, published medians are around 10–11 months. T-cell and less common forms may have a shorter course, while some low-grade forms can be controlled longer. These are group results, not a clock for one dog; type, location, overall health and response to treatment matter.',
'mast cell tumor': 'The outlook for mast cell tumors ranges from excellent to serious. A small low-grade tumor that can be completely removed may be cured with surgery, while high-grade, recurrent, node-positive or metastatic disease needs a more guarded outlook and often additional treatment. Grade, mitotic activity, margins and lymph-node status matter more than one average survival number.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'Many low- and intermediate-grade soft tissue sarcomas can be controlled for a long time when the first local treatment is well planned. Higher grade, large size, recurrence, difficult location and metastatic spread worsen the outlook. For an individual dog, grade and the ability to achieve durable local control are usually more useful than a single survival statistic.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'The outlook depends first on where the tumor started. A tumor limited to the surface of the skin may sometimes be cured with complete removal. For visceral hemangiosarcoma, one retrospective study reported a median of about 2 months after surgery alone and 9 months after surgery followed by doxorubicin. Those figures describe groups, not an individual dog, but they show why surgery may stop the immediate bleeding without curing hidden disease and why an oncology discussion should not be delayed.',
'osteosarcoma': 'For a limb osteosarcoma with no visible spread, historical medians are about 3–4 months after amputation alone and about 9–11 months when platinum-based chemotherapy follows amputation. Some dogs live much longer. Tumors in the spine, skull or pelvis and cancer that is already visible elsewhere do not share those expectations. Pain and fracture risk still need attention immediately.',
'oral melanoma': 'The outlook changes sharply by stage. Published radiation-treated groups reported median survival of about 25 months for stage I, 9 months for stage II, 5 months for stage III and 2–3 months for stage IV. Other treatments and study populations give different results, so these are orientation points rather than a prediction. They show why measuring the tumor and checking lymph nodes and lungs matters before treatment.',
'melanoma': 'The outlook can range from cure after surgery to a cancer that is difficult to control. A completely removed, low-risk skin tumor may never return. Tumors with aggressive pathology, incomplete removal or spread to lymph nodes or lungs need a more guarded outlook and usually more than surgery alone. The pathology report and staging results are what allow the oncologist to place your dog between those two ends of the range.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'Canine oral squamous cell carcinoma is often primarily a local-control problem, and some dogs with tumors that can be completely removed can do well for a long time. Tumor site, size, bone invasion, lymph-node involvement and the ability to obtain clean local control are the major factors that shape prognosis.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'The outlook for squamous cell carcinoma depends heavily on where it starts. A small skin lesion may be curable with local treatment, while tumors in some oral, nasal or other sites can be much more invasive. Site, depth, stage and the ability to obtain local control are more useful than a generic SCC survival number.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'This cancer is usually managed rather than cured. Published treatment studies report median survival ranging roughly from 6 to 18 months, but the groups differed in tumor location and treatment. Disease involving the prostate or blocking urine flow generally carries a worse outlook. Difficulty passing urine is urgent regardless of any survival estimate.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'If there is one removable liver mass and no spread, surgery can be genuinely worthwhile. In a classic surgical study, median survival after liver-lobe removal exceeded 4 years, compared with about 9 months in a small nonsurgical group. Those groups were not equivalent, but they show that a large solitary mass is not automatically hopeless. Multifocal, diffuse or unresectable cancer is a very different situation.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Some dogs are cured by complete surgery, especially when the tumor is found small and pathology shows low-risk features. In one published group, the median was about 3 years when no tumor cells were seen in lymphatic vessels, compared with about 6 months when lymphatic invasion was present. Those figures are not universal, but they show why margins, grade, lymphatic invasion, lymph nodes and chest staging matter.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Many dogs with a localized, movable thyroid tumor can do well for years. A large thyroid-surgery series reported a median of about 26 months, and selected surgically treated dogs have lived beyond three years. A fixed tumor involving major vessels or cancer that has spread is a different situation, so resectability and staging must be known before applying those figures.',
'prostate cancer': 'Published outcomes vary widely because treatment groups are very different. One multi-hospital medical-treatment group had a median survival of about 3 months, while a selected group receiving definitive radiation had a median of about 18 months. That does not mean radiation gives every dog that result. Extent of disease, ability to maintain urine flow and whether effective local treatment is possible determine which comparison is relevant.',
'primary lung tumor': 'A solitary lung tumor that can be completely removed may allow meaningful long-term control. In one contemporary surgical series, dogs without cancer in chest lymph nodes had a median survival of about 15 months, compared with about 5–6 months when nodes were positive; the small study could not prove that difference statistically. Multiple lesions, pleural disease and aggressive pathology also worsen the outlook.',
'glioma': 'In selected dogs treated with modern stereotactic or definitive radiation, published median survival has been roughly 12–23 months. That range does not apply automatically to every MRI-suspected glioma: location, neurologic condition, control of seizures or brain swelling and whether the diagnosis was confirmed all matter. Without local treatment, expectations may be shorter.',
'meningioma': 'Meaningful control is often possible. A 2025 multicenter study reported median survival of about 23 months after radiation and 10 months after surgery, but the dogs selected for those treatments were different, so the figures cannot be used as a direct comparison. Tumor location, neurologic condition and whether effective local control is possible determine which outlook is realistic.',
'nasal tumor': 'For dogs treated with radiation, published median survival is commonly around 8–18 months. The range is wide because biopsy type and local extent differ. In one recent group, the median was about 15 months without destruction of the bone separating the nasal cavity from the brain, versus about 6 months when that barrier was affected. CT and biopsy show whether those figures are relevant.',
'leukemia': 'The realistic range is wide. Fast-moving leukemia can become life-threatening quickly and may be difficult to control even with treatment. Some slower forms can be managed for months or years while the dog still feels well. The first useful answer is therefore not an average survival number, but whether this is a fast or slow form and whether low blood counts already require urgent support.',
'multiple myeloma': 'Many dogs respond to treatment. A classic treated series reported a median survival of about 18 months, with much better outcomes in dogs that responded. Kidney injury, high calcium, severe anemia, abnormal blood proteins and bone damage can make the outlook more serious. The early treatment response is therefore more useful than treating 18 months as a deadline.',
'chemodectoma': 'Some dogs remain comfortable for a long time. Retrospective groups treated with toceranib reported median survival around 16–26 months, while one stereotactic-radiation group reported about 13 months. These were selected dogs, not proof that one treatment is better. Growth, fluid buildup, abnormal heart rhythm, compression and spread determine how relevant those figures are.',
}

CAT = {
'lymphoma': 'Some slow-growing intestinal lymphomas can be controlled for years. Faster-growing forms usually need combination chemotherapy and are less predictable, although many cats improve when treatment works. In one selected group with high-grade gastrointestinal lymphoma treated with surgery and CHOP, the median was about 14 months. That number does not apply to every feline lymphoma; location, grade and early response matter.',
'mast cell tumor': 'A solitary skin mast cell tumor can often be cured with surgery. Splenic disease can also have a meaningful outlook after splenectomy: one study reported median tumor-specific survival of about 28 months with splenectomy versus 11 months without it, while another surgical series reported about 13 months overall. Intestinal, disseminated or recurrent disease is more concerning, and canine grading rules should not be applied to cats.',
'soft tissue sarcoma': 'Many feline soft tissue sarcomas are primarily a local-control problem, but injection-site sarcomas can be especially infiltrative and prone to local recurrence. The best outlook comes when the first definitive treatment is planned widely enough to control microscopic extension. Grade, location, recurrence and metastatic spread also matter.',
'hemangiosarcoma': 'Complete removal may give long control for some tumors confined to the surface of the skin. Visceral disease is very different: a 26-cat series found a median survival of about 2–3 months among the six cats that survived the immediate diagnostic period, and a newer splenectomy group reported about 3 months. These small, selected groups do not predict an individual cat and should never be applied to a completely removed skin tumor.',
'osteosarcoma': 'Cats with a removable bone tumor and no visible spread can sometimes have long-term control after surgery. The outlook is more guarded when the tumor cannot be removed, arises in the spine, skull or pelvis, or has already spread. Feline osteosarcoma often spreads less readily than the canine form, but staging is still necessary and a dog’s survival estimate should not be applied to your cat.',
'oral melanoma': 'The available studies are small, but they consistently describe a serious disease. A 20-cat retrospective group had a median survival of about 3–4 months, and a five-cat radiation group had a median of about 5 months. These figures are not a deadline for one cat, but they support prompt confirmation, staging and treatment discussion rather than watchful waiting.',
'melanoma': 'There is too little feline evidence to give one trustworthy survival estimate. A completely removed tumor with low-risk pathology may be controlled for a long time; an invasive tumor or one that has spread carries a much more guarded outlook. Your cat’s pathology and staging matter, and canine melanoma numbers should not be presented as if they predict a cat’s course.',
'oral squamous cell carcinoma': 'The outlook is unfortunately often guarded. Published groups reported medians of about 1–2 months for mandibular, sublingual and maxillary tumors, about 5 months for oropharyngeal tumors, and about 5 months in one radiation-plus-carboplatin study. A small tumor that can be controlled completely may do much better. These figures explain why pain, eating and an oncology plan need attention now; supplements or home remedies should not delay that discussion.',
'squamous cell carcinoma': 'An early surface lesion that can be completely treated may be cured. In published cutaneous groups, median disease-free time was about 20 months after surgery and 30 months after radiation for nasal-planum tumors; the latter group also had a median overall survival of about 30 months. Those encouraging figures do not apply to oral SCC, which is much harder to control. Site and depth must be clear before discussing expectations.',
'urothelial carcinoma': 'Feline urothelial carcinoma is uncommon, so reliable survival estimates are limited. The immediate outlook depends heavily on tumor location, whether urine flow is threatened, kidney function and whether disease has spread. A favorably located tumor that can be controlled locally is a different situation from obstructive or metastatic disease.',
'hepatocellular carcinoma': 'A cat with one removable liver tumor may do well after surgery. One 19-cat surgical series reported median survival of about 2.4 years, and a larger multicenter group reported a very similar figure. These were selected retrospective populations, not promises. Cancer involving several parts of the liver, important structures or distant organs is much harder to control.',
'mammary carcinoma': 'Early, complete surgery offers the best chance of longer control, so a confirmed or strongly suspected tumor should not simply be watched. Smaller tumors without lymph-node or lung spread generally have better outcomes, but recurrence and spread remain real concerns. After surgery, the pathology report and staging determine whether chemotherapy should be discussed and how closely your cat should be monitored.',
'thyroid carcinoma': 'Feline thyroid carcinoma is rare, so there is no dependable survival number that applies to every cat. Some localized or iodine-responsive tumors can be controlled for a long time, while invasive or metastatic tumors are more difficult. Local invasion, spread, kidney function and whether the tumor is iodine-avid help determine the realistic options and outlook.',
'prostate cancer': 'There is not enough feline evidence for a reliable survival estimate. The immediate outlook is most serious when the tumor interferes with urination or defecation; inability to pass urine is an emergency. If your cat is stable, imaging and staging can show whether local treatment is possible and whether cancer is present elsewhere. Canine prostate-cancer statistics are not a dependable substitute.',
'primary lung tumor': 'A cat with one lung tumor that can be completely removed may have meaningful time after surgery. The outlook is more guarded when there are several lung spots, cancer in nearby lymph nodes or the lining around the lungs, or spread to other organs. Scans and the pathology report give a more useful picture for your cat than one average survival number.',
'glioma': 'Feline glioma is rare, and there is not enough feline evidence for a dependable survival estimate. The outlook depends on tumor location, neurologic condition, control of seizures or brain swelling and whether radiation, surgery or another local treatment is feasible. Canine survival figures should not be substituted for feline data.',
'meningioma': 'This is one brain-tumor diagnosis where treatment can sometimes provide years of good-quality life. A 121-cat multicenter surgical series reported a median survival of 37 months after removal. That favorable figure applies to selected surgical patients, not every cat. Location, neurologic condition and operative risk determine whether it is relevant.',
'nasal tumor': 'The biopsy result changes the outlook because lymphoma and carcinoma do not follow the same course. In one intranasal-carcinoma study, the median was about 24 months after definitive-intent radiation and 9 months after palliative radiation; an older small radiation series reported about 13 months. Those treatment groups were different, so the numbers guide expectations but do not compare the methods fairly.',
'leukemia': 'There is no single outlook for every cat with leukemia. Fast-moving forms can make a cat very ill because the body may stop making enough healthy red cells, platelets or infection-fighting cells. Slower forms can sometimes be controlled for much longer. Your cat’s blood counts, the exact kind of leukemia and how well treatment works will give you a more useful answer than one survival number.',
'multiple myeloma': 'Feline myeloma is uncommon, so the evidence is limited. A 2024 multicenter study reported median survival of about 4 months overall, while older reports include individual cats living well beyond a year. Anemia, low platelets, kidney injury, high calcium and bone disease can shorten the course. The wide range means early treatment response is especially important.',
'chemodectoma': 'Chemodectoma is extremely rare in cats, so there is no reliable feline survival estimate. The practical outlook depends on whether the heart-base mass is growing or causing compression, fluid accumulation, arrhythmia or metastatic spread. Canine treatment outcomes should be treated only as indirect guidance.',
'histiocytic sarcoma': 'Feline histiocytic sarcoma is rare and often aggressive. One study that recorded outcomes reported a median survival of about 5 months, but the group was small. A localized lesion that can be completely controlled may behave very differently from cancer involving several organs. Canine survival figures should not be substituted for better feline evidence.',
}
